I woke before dawn. Not gently   not the slow drift from sleep to consciousness that normal mornings brought. This was a surfacing, fast and desperate, like a drowning man breaking through the surface of black water. My eyes opened and my body was already screaming at me, every nerve ending in every inch of skin firing a message that my brain couldn't parse into language, only sensation.

Tingling. Everywhere. Not the localized pins-and-needles of a limb gone numb. This was systemic, total, a full-body hum that started somewhere deep under my skin and radiated outward through tissue and muscle and nerve. Like being wrapped in a blanket made of static. Like every cell in my body had been replaced overnight by a slightly different cell and the new ones were introducing themselves to the old ones and the conversation was loud.

/* <img src="img/scene/day_4/1.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" /> */
I lay still. Heart hammering. Staring at the ceiling. The water stain shaped like a horse was still there   the one constant in a universe that was becoming increasingly unreliable. The cracks in the plaster. The morning light, not yet arrived, the room still held in the gray predawn light that turned everything to ash and shadow.

Mira was asleep beside me. I could hear her breathing   slow, deep, the half-elf sleep rhythm that was more efficient than human rest, every breath extracting maximum recovery from minimum time. Her back was to me, her cropped copper hair a dark smudge against the pale pillow. One of her hands rested on my hip, fingers curled loosely against the fabric of my smallclothes. A casual touch. Proprietary. The unconscious gesture of a woman who'd claimed territory in her sleep and saw no reason to relinquish it.

I needed the mirror. Carefully   gods, so carefully   I lifted her hand from my hip and slid out from under the blanket. The air hit my skin and the tingling intensified. Not painfully. Differently. The cool morning air against my arms, my chest, my stomach, registered with a vividness that made me catch my breath. Every hair on my forearms was a sensor. Every pore was an antenna. The air wasn't just cold   it was textured, layered, full of information that my skin was suddenly equipped to receive and had no idea how to process.

I crossed the room. Three steps. The floorboards were cold under my bare feet and each plank had its own temperature, its own grain, its own specific geography that the soles of my feet mapped with cartographic precision. I stood in front of the polished tin that served as our mirror and leaned close.

/* <img src="img/scene/day_4/2.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" /> */
The predawn light was thin, gray, unforgiving. The mirror was cheap and warped and showed me an approximation of my face rather than the real thing. But even through the distortion, even in this light, I could see it.

My skin was different. Not subtly different. Not the marginal, debatable softening I'd been tracking for two days, the kind of change you could attribute to imagination or healing compounds or the general unreliability of self-perception. This was visible. Measurable. The weathered, sun-roughened texture of my face   the leathery quality of ten years of outdoor living, the grain of wind and road dust baked into my pores   was simply gone. Replaced by something smoother, cleaner, newer. Like the skin of a man ten years younger. Like the skin of someone who'd never slept rough or sweated under armor or squinted into driving rain on an open road.

I touched my cheek. My fingers met a surface that felt like   I didn't have a reference. Like the inner wrist, maybe. That soft, protected skin that never sees weather or work. Except this was my cheek. My jaw. The parts of my face that had been tanned leather since I was sixteen and started sleeping in fields.

The stubble was worse. Or better, depending on your perspective. Two days ago, three days of growth had produced the thick, dark shadow I'd worn like a uniform for most of my adult life   the perpetual scruff that women either found rugged or slovenly, depending on the woman. Yesterday, it was patchy. Today, it was barely there. A ghost of growth, thin and pale, concentrated on the chin and upper lip and absent entirely along the jaw. I rubbed my fingers across the bare places and felt nothing. Not shaved-smooth   that had a different texture, the freshly razored feeling that came with a blade. This was skin that had simply stopped growing hair. Smooth like it had never known stubble. Like the follicles had packed their things and left without forwarding their address.

