I pulled my undershirt up. The chest was   I'd known it would be worse. I'd felt it in the tingling, in the way the linen registered against my skin with that new, screaming sensitivity. But knowing and seeing were different countries, and the border between them was a mirror at dawn.

/* <img src="img/scene/day_4/3.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" /> */
The chest hair was half gone. Not thinning   departing. The thick dark mat that had covered my pectorals since puberty had been reduced to sparse, fine strands scattered across skin that gleamed in the gray light with a smoothness that looked almost polished. I could see the contours of my pectorals more clearly now   the muscle itself unchanged, still thick, still hard, still the flat slabs I'd built through a decade of swordwork. But the surface had changed. The skin between the muscles, in the valley of my sternum, along the lower edges of the pectorals   it was smooth. Soft. Tender in a way that the word "tender" shouldn't apply to a grown man's chest and the nipples.

I looked at them. They looked back with the mute insistence of body parts that had undergone renovation without consulting the owner. Yesterday they'd been puffy   swollen, sensitive, the areolae wider than normal. Today they were unmistakably different. The areolae had expanded further, the edges of the darker skin spreading outward like ink on wet paper, the color deepening from their usual faded pink to something richer, warmer, a rosy hue that caught the light differently. The nipple itself was larger   not dramatically, not visibly to a casual observer, but I could see it. The small, flat disc I'd lived with for twenty-six years had become a small, raised nub, slightly protruding, sitting at the center of the expanded areola like a button on a cushion.

I touched the right one. Just a fingertip. The lightest possible pressure.

The sensation detonated through my chest like a shockwave. Electric   no. More than electric. A jolt that started at the contact point and radiated outward in concentric rings, crashing through my pectorals, my sternum, my stomach, my groin, my spine. Every nerve in my torso fired simultaneously, a cascade of signals that my brain translated into a sensation somewhere between a gasp and a scream   pleasure and alarm so tangled together that I couldn't separate them, couldn't tell where one ended and the other began.

My back arched. Involuntarily. My hand jerked away from my chest as if the nipple had burned me, and a sound escaped my lips   a sharp, high-pitched "aah!" that I bit off halfway through, my teeth clamping down on my lower lip with enough force to taste copper.

That sound. That was not my sound. That was not the grunt or groan or quiet inhale that touching my chest had ever produced before. That was a sound from a different register, a different body, a different person.

I pressed my hand flat against my chest. Both hands. Palms covering the nipples, fingers spread across the pectorals, holding the changed flesh down like I could hold it still, like I could keep it from becoming what it was becoming. Under my palms, I could feel it   the warmth of accelerated change, the subtle shift of tissue reorganizing itself beneath the muscle. Not breasts. Not yet. But the ground being prepared. The foundation being laid. The early architecture of something that my hands knew was coming even if my brain refused to name it.

My waist. I moved my hands down, pressing inward on both sides in the gesture that had become my daily inventory   the width check, the measurement of a distance I knew by muscle memory. Belt sits here. Leather wraps here. This is how wide I am.

The distance was smaller. Not by a fraction this time. Not by a coin's width or a finger's span. By an inch, maybe more, the narrowing visible even through the undershirt when I looked down at myself. My waist had cinched inward overnight like a corset tightening in slow motion, the muscles of my obliques pulling closer together, the natural taper of a male torso deepening into something more pronounced, more dramatic, a curvature that I could feel under my fingertips and could see in the mirror's warped reflection.

I yanked the undershirt down. Pushed my smallclothes lower on my hips   my hips, which ached with that deep bone-throb I'd first noticed two days ago, worse now, a grinding awareness of skeletal change happening beneath the skin. I pressed my fingertips against the hip bones and  

Wider. Wider by enough that I could feel the difference in the way my smallclothes sat, the waistband that used to ride comfortably now pinching at the sides where the bones had pushed outward. Not dramatically. Not visibly through clothing. But I could feel it. My hands could map the new geography, and the geography was wrong, was changing, was reshaping the framework of my body from the inside out.

