She turned away. Started dressing   vest, belt, bow, the systematic donning of her professional identity that she performed with the same mechanical efficiency whether the job ahead was clearing goblins or confronting witches. Each piece of gear locked into place with the soft clicks and buckle-sounds of a woman arming herself for battle.

I got dressed. The tunic was   different. The wool settled against my chest and the fabric found the nipples and the sensation was a sustained, low-level assault that made my breath come shallow and my teeth set. Every fiber was a conversation with nerve endings that had been rewired overnight, and the conversation was loud and confusing and I couldn't turn the volume down.

My belt buckled at the same notch but the fit was wrong. Looser at the waist, tighter at the hips, the leather sitting at an angle it had never sat before. My trousers   fine through the legs, but pulling at the hips in a way that was new, the side seams straining slightly against bone that was wider than the fabric had been cut to accommodate.

I looked at myself in the mirror one more time. Clothed, the changes were   manageable. Barely visible. The stubble situation was the most obvious   anyone who knew what my jaw normally looked like would notice the patchy, thin growth that was more absence than presence. But the rest? The smoother skin could be attributed to a good wash. The slightly different silhouette was hidden by the loose cut of the tunic. The belt rode a little oddly but nobody examined another man's belt with forensic attention. Nobody was going to notice. Not today. Not yet.
