:: day_4_3
<<set $day_4_3 = true>>
The Undercity at dawn was becoming familiar, which was not a state of affairs I'd ever anticipated or desired. Two visits in three days to the cistern district   the dampest, deepest, most questionable corner of Valdmere's most questionable quarter   and I was starting to recognize landmarks. The chandler's shutters with the broken hinge. The mystery fermentation building that smelled like someone was distilling regret. The particular shade of mold on the stone walls that started about twenty paces from Ysolde's door and deepened as you approached, as if the stone itself was reacting to whatever alchemical work happened inside.

/* <img src="img/scene/day_4/5.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" /> */
My skin prickled the entire walk down. Every gust of damp air through the narrow Undercity alleys hit me like a caress   not painful, but intensely present. The rough wool of my tunic against my chest was a sustained conversation that I couldn't mute, the fabric talking to nipples that were determined to listen to everything. I caught myself hunching   arms crossed, shoulders rounded, the defensive posture of a man protecting something he didn't want to name.

Mira walked beside me in silence. She'd said nothing since we left the Broken Antler. Not angry silence   working silence. The quiet of a woman running scenarios, calculating approaches, preparing arguments for a conversation that hadn't happened yet. Her amber eyes were forward, her stride purposeful, and her hand rested on the pommel of the cheese-smelling short sword, not because she expected combat but because touching her weapon was how Mira self-soothed, the way another person might wring their hands or bite their nails.

We reached the door. The same unremarkable wooden door in the same stone wall. I knocked. Three, then two, then one. The pattern had become automatic. Muscle memory forming around a ritual I hadn't asked to learn.

Silence. I knocked again. Harder. The wood rattled in its frame. More silence. No footsteps. No bolt drawing back. No chain rattling. No clinking of tiny glass vials on a slender wrist.

