We waited. The cellar was cold without the oil lamps lit. Mira found a tinderbox on a shelf and lit two of them, the warm amber glow pushing back the underground dark and filling the room with the smell of burning oil and the faint, lingering scent of Ysolde's herbs   lavender, sage, something sharper. The smell of the woman who'd done this to me, embedded in the stone walls like a ghost.

I sat on the heavy wooden table. The same table she'd laid me on three nights ago when she'd extracted eight splinters from my calf and stitched the wound and poured clear liquid that burned and amber liquid that numbed and then handed me a small vial of green-black liquid that moved like something alive and said drink this and I had. Because Mira told me to. Because I was in pain. Because I trusted a woman I didn't know in a cellar I'd never been to, and the trust had cost me my body.

