She picked up the pot of wound salve Ysolde had left. Opened it. Sniffed it   cautiously, the way you'd sniff something left by a person you no longer trusted. Her nose wrinkled.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Standard wound salve. Comfrey, beeswax, calendula. Nothing suspicious. She left this for us deliberately   the only compound in the room I can fully identify. Whatever else she took with her, she left the one thing that's just medicine.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Generous of her. After she poisoned me.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We don't know it's poison.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What would you call it?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'd call it something I don't have a word for yet. Poison kills you. Whatever this is   it's changing you. Those are different verbs.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Comforting distinction.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm not trying to comfort you. I'm trying to be accurate. Comfort comes after information. Information comes from the apprentice. The apprentice comes at mid-morning. It's currently early morning. Patience.
<</dialog>>

I did not feel patient. I felt like a man sitting in the workshop of the person who'd disassembled his life, waiting for a stranger to explain the instruction manual. The door opened at the fifth hour.

[[Meet Nella|day_4_4_1]]

:: day_4_4_1
/* <img src="img/scene/day_4/7.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" /> */
The woman who entered was young   early twenties, maybe. Small, dark-haired, with the compact build of someone who spent their days on their feet in close quarters. She wore a healer's apron over a plain linen dress, and her hands were already stained   green and blue at the fingertips, the occupational tattoo of someone who worked with compounds. Her face was round, pleasant, unremarkable in every way except for the eyes   dark, watchful, with a quick intelligence that reminded me uncomfortably of Ysolde.

She saw us and stopped. Her gaze went to Mira   armed, alert, leaning against the wall like a weapon in storage   and then to me, sitting on the table with my arms crossed over my chest.

Her eyes lingered on me. On my face. On the too-smooth jaw, the near-absent stubble, the skin that gleamed in the lamplight with its new, unwanted softness. She studied me the way Ysolde had   the professional assessment, the diagnostic scan   but where Ysolde's examination had been opaque, unreadable, this woman's reaction was transparent. Her brow furrowed. Her lips pressed together. Something that looked like recognition moved across her face.

<<dialog "Nella">>
You're the one she told me about. The sellsword. The one who drank the   yes. I can see it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
See what?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
The changes. They're further along than I expected at   how many days?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Three. Three nights since I drank the vial.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
Three days. That's   fast. Ysolde said the initial phase usually takes five to seven days before visible changes manifest. You're running ahead of schedule.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Ahead of schedule for what?
<</dialog>>

Nella set her bag on the smaller table and began unpacking   bottles, instruments, a leather journal that she opened to a marked page. She moved with the practiced efficiency of someone who'd assisted in this space many times, her hands finding the right shelves without looking.

<<dialog "Nella">>
I need to examine you. Properly. Can you remove your tunic?
<</dialog>>

[[Remove tunic|day_4_5]]

:: day_4_5

I pulled the tunic over my head. The air hit my bare chest and the nipples tightened immediately   not the mild puckering of cold skin, but a sharp, almost painful contraction that sent sensation radiating through my torso. I gritted my teeth and set the tunic aside.

Nella's hands were careful. Professional. She pressed fingertips against my jaw, my neck, my collarbones   tracing the bone structure, testing the skin texture, mapping the geography of a body in transition. She checked my pulse at the wrist. Examined my hands   the thinning calluses, the smoother palms. Ran a finger along my forearm and frowned at the sparse, fine hair.

<<dialog "Nella">>
Can I examine the chest?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Touch the nipples and I might hit you. Fair warning.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
Sensitivity is expected. I'll be careful.
<</dialog>>

She was. Her fingertips pressed the pectoral muscle   testing density, mass, the quality of the tissue underneath. She noted the near-absence of chest hair, the smooth skin, the expanded areolae. She cupped one hand beneath the left pectoral and pressed upward, gently, and I felt the faintest give   not muscle giving way, but something softer beneath the muscle, a thin layer of new tissue that moved when she pressed it.

<<dialog "Nella">>
Tissue development has begun behind the pectorals. It's very early   maybe two millimeters of glandular growth. You probably can't see it yet but you can feel it. A fullness. A tenderness.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I can feel it.
<</dialog>>

She moved lower. Pressed against my waist   the narrowing. Pressed against my hips   the widening. She measured with her hands, then with a cloth tape she pulled from her bag, recording numbers in the leather journal with quick, precise notations.

<<dialog "Nella">>
Turn around.
<</dialog>>

I turned. Her hands mapped my back   the shoulder blades, still broad, still muscular. The spine. The narrowing waist from behind. The slight new curve at the hips that she measured and noted.

<<dialog "Nella">>
Turn back. I need to check the lower body. Can you  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
She doesn't need to see my  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Yes, she does. If you want answers, she needs complete data.
<</dialog>>

I lowered my trousers. Stood in the cold cellar in my smallclothes, which was a dignity I held onto like a drowning man holding a plank. Nella examined my thighs   the subtle softening, the finer hair. She pressed against the hip bones, feeling the width, the angle, the slow lateral migration of the skeleton.

<<dialog "Nella">>
You can dress.
<</dialog>>

I dressed. Fast. The tunic went on and the wool found the nipples and I winced but said nothing. Nella was writing in her journal, filling half a page with notes, her handwriting as precise as Ysolde's but smaller, tighter, the compact script of someone conserving paper.

When she finished, she closed the journal. Looked at me. Then at Mira. Then back at me.

<<dialog "Nella">>
I'm going to explain what's happening. You're not going to like it. I need you to let me finish before you react.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Tell me.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
The compound Ysolde gave you is called a feminization agent. It's an alchemical formula that induces a complete biological transition from male to female. Skeletal, muscular, dermal, hormonal, and reproductive. Full systemic change over a thirty-day cycle.
<</dialog>>

The words hit me in sequence, each one a separate blow. Feminization. Complete. Male to female. Full. Thirty days.

<<dialog "Nella">>
The changes you're experiencing   the skin softening, the hair reduction, the nipple sensitivity, the waist narrowing, the hip widening   these are early-phase effects. The compound is rewriting your body's blueprint. Over the next twenty-seven days, every system will be affected. Bone structure. Fat distribution. Muscle mass. Skin texture. Hair growth patterns. Voice and ultimately   fully   reproductive anatomy.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Reproductive  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
By Day 30, if the cycle completes without intervention, you will have a fully female body. External and internal. Complete.
<</dialog>>

The silence that followed was the loudest sound I'd ever heard. Mira's hand found my shoulder. Her grip was iron. I didn't know if she was holding me up or holding me down.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Reverse it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
I can't. The formula for the anti-compound exists, but only Ysolde knows it. I've assisted her for three years and she never shared the reversal process. She kept it   she keeps all of her most sensitive work   to herself.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
She said in the note that reversal is possible at any stage.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
It is. The compound isn't permanent. It's not a one-way door. Even after the thirty-day cycle completes and the body has fully stabilized as female, the anti-compound can reverse the changes. The transformation locks in, yes   it doesn't continue changing or destabilize. But it's reversible. At any point. Before Day 30. After Day 30. A year after Day 30.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Then why can't you  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
Because I don't have the formula. Only Ysolde does. Without her, or without the anti-compound itself, the changes remain. Not because they're permanent in principle   because the one person who can undo them has left.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
She left a note. Thornfield Priory, five days northeast. Blackthorn Market, seven days.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
That sounds right. She has connections at both. But I can't tell you which one she'll be at, or if she'll stay long enough for you to catch her. Ysolde moves when she wants to move. She doesn't wait for anyone.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Does she do this often? Give people compounds that   transform them?
<</dialog>>

Nella hesitated. The first crack in her professional composure. She looked down at her journal, then up at Mira, and the expression on her face was complicated   guilt, maybe. Or the echo of guilt. The awareness that she worked for a woman who did things that required this conversation.

<<dialog "Nella">>
I've seen the compound twice before. Both times, the recipients came to Ysolde specifically seeking it. They knew what it was. They requested it. They took it willingly.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I didn't request it. I didn't know what it was. I thought it was a healing draught for a wound infection.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
I know. That's   that's not how it's supposed to work. Ysolde has always insisted on informed consent. What she did with you   I don't understand it. She's never  
<</dialog>>

She stopped herself. Swallowed something she'd been about to say. Smoothed the page of her journal with one stained fingertip.

<<dialog "Nella">>
I don't understand why she gave you the feminization compound instead of the standard healing agent. They look similar   both are green, both come in the same style of vial. But the formulations are entirely different. Maybe she grabbed the wrong one. Maybe  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
She said "faster than I expected." When we came back yesterday. Those were her words. She expected the changes. She knew.
<</dialog>>

Nella said nothing. Her silence was its own kind of answer.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What happens if I do nothing? If I don't find her?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
The cycle completes. Day 30, your body stabilizes as fully female. You live as a woman. It's not dangerous   the compound doesn't damage organs or cause disease. The change is healthy. Biologically functional. You'd be a healthy woman. Just   not the person you were.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
A healthy woman.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
With every physical capability intact. The process doesn't diminish you. It   redirects you.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Redirects.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
I'm sorry. I know this isn't  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Helpful? No.
<</dialog>>

Mira's grip on my shoulder tightened. She pulled me toward the door, gently but with the non-negotiable pressure of a woman who'd decided it was time to leave.

<<dialog "Mira">>
What can you do for him? Practically. Right now.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
I can provide a schedule. Based on the previous cases I've observed, I can tell you approximately what to expect each day   what changes, what sequence, what timeline. So nothing catches you off guard and I can give you compounds for comfort   pain management for the skeletal changes, something for the skin sensitivity, a salve that will help with the   the chest tenderness.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Do it. All of it. How much?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
Four coppers. For everything.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
That's   nothing.
<</dialog>>

[[Pay Nella four coppers|day_4_5_1]]

:: day_4_5_1

<<dialog "Nella">>
Consider it a professional courtesy. For what my mentor did to your partner.
<</dialog>>

She prepared a package   small bottles labeled in her precise hand, a tin of salve, a folded parchment with a day-by-day timeline written in dark ink. She handed it to Mira, not me. A choice that I noticed and understood   she was giving the supplies to the one of us who could be trusted not to throw them against a wall.

I sat on the table. Mira stood beside me. The information settled between us like sediment in still water   heavy, slow, finding the bottom.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
She did this on purpose.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
I don't know her reasons. I've worked with Ysolde for three years and she's never   she's deliberate. Everything she does is deliberate. But this   giving the feminization compound to someone who didn't ask for it   that's outside anything I've seen from her.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The timeline. Walk us through it. Day by day. What should he expect?
<</dialog>>

Nella opened the leather journal to a section dense with notes   tables, measurements, a hand-drawn diagram of a human figure marked with numbered zones. She turned it to face us.

<<dialog "Nella">>
The compound works in waves. Not linear   waves. Some days the changes will be dramatic. Other days they'll plateau. The general sequence is: surface first, then structure, then organs.
<</dialog>>

She pointed to the diagram. Numbers and annotations covered the figure like a medical text.

<<dialog "Nella">>
You're in the surface phase now. Skin texture, hair reduction, enhanced sensitivity. These continue and accelerate through Day 10. By Day 7, your stubble will be entirely gone. Your body hair will be reduced to fine vellus fuzz   the kind of hair that children have, barely visible. Your calluses will be gone. Your skin will be uniformly smooth.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
My calluses. My sword calluses.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
Your grip strength won't change significantly until the second phase. You'll still be able to hold a sword. But the protective tissue on your palms will thin. You'll need to wrap the grip   leather binding, cloth padding. Something between your skin and the steel.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What else?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
The structural phase overlaps with the surface phase. It's already started   the waist narrowing, the hip widening. That accelerates. By Day 10, the skeletal changes will be noticeable to observant people even through clothing. Your waist will narrow by two to three inches total. Your hips will widen by roughly the same. The visual effect creates a curvature. A taper. The body begins to read as differently proportioned even before the more dramatic changes arrive.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
More dramatic changes.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
Breast development begins in earnest around Day 5 to Day 7. Right now you have preliminary tissue   maybe two millimeters. By Day 10, you'll have visible growth. Small   barely filling a cupped hand   but present. By Day 15, a full A-cup. By Day 20, approaching B. By Day 25  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Stop. I don't   I can't hear the full list right now.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
I understand. I'll write it all down. You can read it when you're ready. The important thing is: nothing that happens will hurt you. The bone aches come and go. The skin sensitivity settles down as the nerve endings stabilize. The tissue growth creates tenderness that fades as the tissue matures. Physically, you'll be fine. This compound doesn't damage the body. It reshapes it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Reshapes. That's one word for it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
There are others. I thought you'd prefer the clinical one.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You mentioned reproductive anatomy. That's the last phase?
<</dialog>>

Nella hesitated. Glanced at me. Reading the room, deciding how much truth the room could hold.

<<dialog "Nella">>
Genital changes begin around Day 8 to Day 10. They're gradual. Slower than the other changes. The existing anatomy reduces. Slowly. It's not sudden and it's not complete until the very end of the cycle. You'll retain function for most of the process. The compound transitions rather than removes. The tissue is restructured. It becomes something different rather than disappearing.
<</dialog>>

The silence that followed was thick enough to drown in.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
My cock.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
Will gradually  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I need you to say it plainly. Without the clinical language. Without the careful phrasing. What happens to my cock?
<</dialog>>

Nella met my eyes. She was braver than she looked, this apprentice. Brave enough to answer the question a man shouldn't have to ask.

<<dialog "Nella">>
It reduces over days 8 through 25. Slowly. The tissue doesn't vanish   it's repurposed. The nerve endings are preserved and concentrated. By Day 30, you'll have female anatomy. Complete and functional. The sensitivity   the nerve density   is actually higher than before. The compound concentrates what was spread across a larger area into a smaller, more sensitive structure.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I heard you.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
I'm sorry.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Don't be sorry. Just   the anti-compound. Ysolde has it. You're sure you can't recreate it?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
I've seen the base ingredients. I know some of the preliminary steps. But the key catalyzing agent   the component that actually reverses the effects   that's Ysolde's proprietary formula. She developed it herself and she keeps it locked in her memory. I could spend years trying to reverse-engineer it and still not get it right. An incorrect reversal compound could be genuinely dangerous. The only safe path is the original formula and the only person with the original formula  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Is five days northeast and running.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
Yes.
<</dialog>>

Nella packed the supplies she'd prepared   the comfort compounds, the schedule, the salve. She handed them to Mira, who accepted them with a nod that communicated gratitude and threat in equal measure   thank you for the help, and if your mentor's formula turns out to be anything less than reversible, I will find you.

At the door, Nella stopped.

<<dialog "Nella">>
One more thing. The dreams.
<</dialog>>

I froze. My hand on the doorframe.

<<dialog "Nella">>
You've been having dreams. Vivid ones. The compound affects the sleeping brain   it sends previews. Images of what the body is becoming. They can be intense. Sometimes erotic. Sometimes frightening. They're not prophecy. They're the brain processing the signals the compound is sending, trying to integrate changes that the conscious mind is resisting.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
They feel real.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
They are real. They're your brain's way of meeting the person you're becoming before your body finishes the introduction. Don't fight them. The dreams that get fought become worse.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Worse how?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
More vivid. More disorienting. More insistent. The compound is designed for willing recipients. It's trying to help you adjust. If you resist, it pushes harder.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Help me adjust to losing my body.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
Help you adjust to gaining a new one. Perspective depends on where you're standing and where you're standing is changing whether you like it or not.
<</dialog>>

[[Nella leaves|day_4_5_2]]

:: day_4_5_2

She left. The door closed behind her. The cellar was quiet   just the sound of water running beneath the floor and the faint clink of remaining bottles on half-empty shelves and two people standing in the workshop of the woman who'd rewritten one of their bodies, trying to figure out how to write it back.

<<dialog "Mira">>
We need to eat.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'm not hungry.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Your body is consuming enormous energy to restructure itself at a cellular level. You need calories the way a forge needs coal. You're going to eat if I have to hold you down and funnel stew into your face. Move.
<</dialog>>

<<addobjective "day_4" "Process the revelation and prepare for tomorrow's escort">>
[[Go back|day_4_6]]

:: day_4_6

We walked back through the Undercity in silence. The morning crowd was thicker now   laborers, early shopkeepers, a woman arguing with a man about the price of dried fish with the passionate intensity of someone negotiating a peace treaty. Normal life. Ordinary transactions. People whose bodies were the same today as yesterday and would be the same tomorrow and who had no idea what a luxury that was.

Mira didn't speak until we were climbing the steps back to the Market District, the damp Undercity air giving way to the dry autumn cold of the upper city.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Thornfield Priory. Five days northeast.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I know.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The Ashenmoor Road runs east. The surveyor's route and the road to Thornfield branches northeast at the Corsham crossroads.
<</dialog>>

I stopped. Looked at her. The morning light caught her face   the sharp angles, the amber eyes, the expression of a woman who was already three moves ahead and waiting for the rest of the board to catch up.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The crossroads is   what, a day and a half east?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Day and a half. The surveyor's ruins site is a half-day past the crossroads. We escort Marsh to his ruins, wait for his assessment, and then   instead of coming straight back   we take the northeast fork. Thornfield is three and a half days from the crossroads. We do both. The escort earns us the money to fund the search, and the route overlaps.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
We'd be gone a week. More.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Eight days, by my calculation. Rough ones. But we clear the Consortium debt, earn our silver, and track down Ysolde in a single trip.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
If she's not at Thornfield? If she's at Blackthorn Market?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Blackthorn is two days further northeast from Thornfield. If she's not at the priory, we push on. Ten days total. It's not ideal. But it's the only plan that doesn't require us to choose between the money and the cure.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
In eight to ten days, I'll be   the changes will be  
<</dialog>>

I stopped myself. Did the math that my body was already doing without my permission. Three days in and the changes were skin-deep   stubble, body hair, sensitivity, minor structural shifts. What would Day 10 look like? Day 12?

Nella's timeline was in Mira's vest pocket. I didn't want to read it. I didn't want to know.

<<dialog "Mira">>
We'll manage. One day at a time. Right now, we need lunch. You haven't eaten since yesterday. Your body is burning energy on   whatever it's doing   and starving yourself won't help.
<</dialog>>
<<set $day_4_6_1 = false>>
<<addobjective "day_4" "Eat lunch at the Broken Antler">>
<<link "Go to the Broken Antler">>
    <<regionmap "valdmere">>
<</link>>

:: day_4_6_1
<<set $day_4_6_1 = true>>
The Broken Antler was half-full with the late-morning crowd   the transition shift between the breakfast stragglers and the lunch regulars, when the common room was mostly dock workers drinking between shifts and a few merchants' apprentices pretending to work while they ate pies. The innkeeper set two bowls of stew in front of us without being asked. The stew was the same as always   gray meat, brown liquid, root vegetables that had surrendered their will to live. I ate it. I tasted it with the new, amplified sensitivity   each ingredient distinct, each flavor more vivid, the salt sharper, the meat gamier, the herbs greener. My tongue was becoming an instrument of greater resolution, which was a genuinely cruel thing to be when the only food available was the Broken Antler's stew.

/* <img src="img/scene/day_4/8.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" /> */
Mira ate in silence for three bites. Then:

<<dialog "Mira">>
Your last week of drinking without worrying about breast tenderness.
<</dialog>>

I choked on the stew. Actually choked   a root vegetable went down the wrong pipe and I coughed into my fist while Mira watched with the satisfied expression of a woman who'd timed her joke perfectly.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That's not funny.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
It's a little funny.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
My body is being   I'm being transformed into a   and you're making breast jokes.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm making breast jokes because if I think about the actual magnitude of what's happening to you, I'll be scared and scared doesn't help. Scared paralyzes. Jokes keep me moving. If you'd prefer, I can sit here in grim silence and stare into the middle distance while you eat your stew in existential terror. But I don't think that's better for either of us.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'd prefer  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
What? What would you prefer? A week ago, we were broke mercenaries eating bad stew and worrying about the rent. Now we're broke mercenaries eating bad stew and worrying about the rent and also your body is turning into a woman's. The stew hasn't changed. The rent hasn't changed. We've just added another item to the list of problems. That's how life works. Problems don't queue politely.
<</dialog>>

She pointed her spoon at me. A root vegetable fragment clung to the end of it like a small, defiant flag.

<<dialog "Mira">>
So here's what we do. We eat. We confirm the escort with Voss. We buy supplies. We leave tomorrow morning. We do the job, we earn the money, we find Ysolde, and we fix this and along the way, I will make exactly as many breast jokes as I feel are necessary to keep us both sane. Objections?
<</dialog>>

I looked at her. The amber eyes, bright with the particular energy she generated when she had a plan and the plan was complicated and she was going to execute it whether the universe cooperated or not. The cropped copper hair, catching the morning light from the window. The spoon with its root vegetable flag, pointed at my chest like a tiny weapon.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I want to kill you.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You always want to kill me. It's part of my charm. Eat your stew.
<</dialog>>

I ate the stew. She was right. The stew hadn't changed. The rent hadn't changed. Only I was changing, one cell at a time, a slow-motion demolition of everything I knew about my own body conducted by a green vial I'd drunk in a cellar because a woman I trusted told me to.

A woman who was now making breast jokes about it and who was, despite everything, the only person in the world who was going to help me fix it.

I finished the stew. It was terrible. The company was better.
<<set $day_4_7 = false>>
<<addobjective "day_4" "Confirm the escort with Voss and collect the advance">>
<<link "Go to the Consortium">>
    <<regionmap "valdmere">>
<</link>>

:: day_4_7
<<set $day_4_7 = true>>
The Consortium warehouse on Dockside Row was exactly as unwelcoming as it had been yesterday   old stone, iron doors, the faded scales-and-wheel emblem, the two bored guards who were paid to look threatening and achieved approximately 60 percent compliance. The same clerk at the same desk sorted us into the same category   actively annoying   and waved us upstairs.

Voss was behind her desk. The ledger was open. The quill was moving. The ink-stained fingers performed their metronomic arithmetic. She didn't look up.

<<dialog "Voss">>
You're early. The surveyor doesn't depart until tomorrow.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We want to confirm the contract and collect the advance. Five silver each, as agreed.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Voss">>
The advance is contingent on departure. You'll receive it tomorrow morning when the surveyor's transport is loaded and the escort begins officially. That's procedure.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We need it today. Travel preparations. Supplies. Equipment adjustments.
<</dialog>>

Voss looked up. Her spectacles caught the lamp light. Behind them, her eyes performed the same three-second assessment they'd performed yesterday   face, body, weapons, calculation. Her gaze lingered on me longer than it had before. On my face, specifically. On the jaw that was smoother than yesterday. On the skin that caught the light differently.

<<dialog "Voss">>
Mr. Vane. You look   rested.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Good night's sleep.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Voss">>
Indeed. Your complexion is improved. Less weathered than when I saw you yesterday. Almost as if you've been using a restorative treatment of some kind.
<</dialog>>

Her tone was neutral. Conversational. The tone of someone making an observation, not an accusation. But Voss didn't make idle observations. Every word she spoke was entered into an internal ledger and balanced against something.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The healer's compounds. For the leg. Side effects.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Voss">>
Side effects. How interesting. One doesn't usually list "improved skin texture" as a side effect of wound treatment. In my experience, side effects tend toward the unpleasant   nausea, headaches, the occasional bout of visions. But improved complexion? That's almost a recommendation.
<</dialog>>

She let the observation sit. Then she opened a drawer, counted ten silver coins   five and five   and placed them on the desk.

<<dialog "Voss">>
Advance payment. Sign here.
<</dialog>>

We signed. Mira's handwriting was precise and elegant. Mine was, as she'd accurately described, like a drunk spider in an inkwell.

<<dialog "Voss">>
The surveyor will meet you at the east gate at first light tomorrow. His name is Hadden Marsh. He's a Consortium assessor   competent at his work, less so at everything else. Protect him. Deliver him to the site. Wait. Deliver him back. Simple.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We have a request regarding the route.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Voss">>
I suspected you might.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
After the assessment is complete and Marsh is delivered back, we'll need to extend our travel northeast. Personal business. Thornfield Priory. Five days from the Corsham crossroads.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Voss">>
Personal business.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Unrelated to the Consortium. We deliver Marsh, complete the escort, and then continue northeast on our own coin.
<</dialog>>

Voss considered this. The spectacles came off. The cleaning cloth came out. The lenses were inspected, polished, reinspected. The ritual of deliberate thought.

<<dialog "Voss">>
The contract specifies escort to the ruins site and return to Valdmere. If you extend beyond the return, the contract timeline shifts. I'll need the assessment data from Marsh delivered at the crossroads rather than at the warehouse. I can arrange a courier pickup at the Corsham waystation.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
That works.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Voss">>
Good. I'll note the adjustment. The Consortium appreciates flexibility   it's one of the few things that doesn't appear on a balance sheet but should. Was there anything else?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The surveyor. Marsh. What's he like?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Voss">>
Fastidious. Anxious. Competent within his domain and completely helpless outside it. He catalogues sites with meticulous precision and panics at unfamiliar noises. He'll require patience, Mr. Vane. Which, based on our limited acquaintance, may not be your strongest asset.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I can be patient.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Voss">>
I look forward to being proven wrong.
<</dialog>>

She put her spectacles back on. Opened the ledger. The quill resumed its metronomic scratch. We'd been processed again.

[[Leave the Consortium|day_4_7_1]]


:: day_4_7_1
On the stairs, Mira pocketed her five silver and exhaled slowly.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Voss noticed your skin.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I know.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
She's not going to say anything. She's not the type. But she noticed. Which means other people will notice. We need to get ahead of it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Get ahead of what? "Excuse me, my face is getting smoother because I accidentally drank a sex-change potion from a witch"?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
"He's using a healing compound for a wound. Side effects include improved complexion." You already said it. That's the story. Keep it simple. People don't question simple.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That works for the skin. What happens when it goes further? When the   when things start becoming  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We'll deal with "further" when "further" arrives. Today, we deal with today.
<</dialog>>

<<addobjective "day_4" "Buy travel supplies at the market">>
<<set $day_4_8 = false>>
<<link "Go to Market District">>
<<regionmap "valdmere">>
<</link>>

:: day_4_8
<<set $day_4_8 = true>>

The Market District was at its loudest in the early afternoon   the post-lunch surge when housewives and cooks descended on the stalls for evening provisions, and the merchants compensated for the competition by shouting their wares at volumes that would impress a town crier. Fish. Bread. Leather goods. A man selling "medicinal" teas from a cart that smelled like a garden had been set on fire.

/* <img src="img/scene/day_4/9.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" /> */
Mira navigated it with the efficiency of a woman who treated shopping the way she treated combat   with a plan, a budget, and no tolerance for deviation. She moved from stall to stall with purpose, buying what she'd already mentally listed: dried meat, hard biscuit, a bag of oats, two waterskins, a coil of rope, a small bottle of lamp oil. Each purchase was evaluated, haggled, and completed in under two minutes. The sellers barely had time to begin their pitches before Mira had named her price, stated her terms, and was walking away with the goods.

I followed. Carrying things. This was my role in the shopping dynamic   the mule. The large man who held the bags while the smaller, sharper woman did the actual work. It was a division of labor I'd accepted with the good grace of someone who recognized his limitations.

My body felt different carrying the supplies. Not weaker   the strength was still there, the arms and shoulders still capable of bearing weight without complaint. But the balance was off. Subtly. The bags hung differently from hands that were minutely smaller, swung differently against hips that were fractionally wider. The center of gravity   that invisible point around which the body organizes itself   had shifted. Lower. Toward the pelvis. I was carrying the same weight but my body was distributing it differently, and the difference registered in my legs, my spine, my posture. I kept adjusting. Widening my stance. Shifting the bags from hand to hand.

Mira noticed. She noticed everything. But she said nothing until the sixth stall, where she stopped in front of a clothing vendor and began rifling through a stack of men's tunics.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I have a tunic.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You have a tunic that fits the body you had yesterday. Based on the rate of change we've observed, by Day 5 or 6, it won't hang correctly. The shoulders will be too wide. The chest will be   the fit will change. We need something looser. More forgiving.
<</dialog>>

She selected a tunic. Dark gray, linen, cut generously through the body with none of the tailored fit that most traveling gear had. It was the kind of garment that would hide changes   loose enough to accommodate shifts in proportion, dark enough to conceal the details.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
It's a tent.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
It's practical and it was two coppers. Put it in the bag.
<</dialog>>

She bought a second one and a set of linen smallclothes   the loose, drawstring kind that would accommodate a changing waist without pinching and   I watched this purchase with the particular helplessness of a man watching his future be planned by someone who'd accepted it faster than he had   a broad linen chest wrap.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That's a  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
It's a bandage. Versatile. Can be used as a wound dressing, a sling, a tourniquet, or   if needed   support.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Support for what?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
For whatever needs supporting. Don't make me say it, Aldric. We both know what the timeline looks like. Being prepared is not the same as being defeated.
<</dialog>>

She paid. Tucked the wrap into the bottom of the supply bag. Didn't look at me while she did it.

The afternoon light was amber and warm, the sun dropping toward the western rooftops, painting the market in the golden glow that made even Valdmere's grubbiest districts look almost romantic. We walked back to the Broken Antler loaded with supplies, our pockets lighter by eight silver but heavy with everything we'd need for a week on the road.

My shadow on the cobblestones looked the same. Broad-shouldered. Male silhouette. Everything correct.

I kept looking at it, though. Checking. Measuring the width of the shoulders against the narrowing waist. Watching for a change in the outline that would announce to the world what only my mirror and Mira and Nella and a departed witch currently knew.

The shadow kept its shape. For now.

[[Return to the Broken Antler|day_4_8b]]

:: day_4_8b

Mira went back to the Consortium warehouse to finalize the route details with Voss. She'd be gone an hour, she said. Maybe more. Voss was thorough and thoroughness took time.

Which left me alone. In the room. With the mirror.

I shouldn't have done what I did. I knew it while I was doing it   the specific self-awareness of a person engaging in behavior they've been warned against, even as their hands are already moving. But the body was demanding to be investigated. The morning's inventory had been interrupted by Mira's waking, by the argument, by the walk to the Undercity and Nella's clinical litany of changes to come. I hadn't had time to properly   to fully  

I locked the door. Pulled the shutters closed. The room went dim, the afternoon light reduced to thin golden lines between the slats, enough to see by but not bright enough to be merciless.

I stripped. Everything. Tunic over the head, trousers down, smallclothes off. Stood naked in the center of the room and looked at the mirror.

/* <img src="img/scene/day_4/10b.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" /> */
The cheap polished tin showed me a warped version of myself   but even through the distortion, the changes assembled into something undeniable. I turned slowly. Front. Side. Other side. Cataloguing the way Mira would catalogue, the way Nella had catalogued, turning my own body into data.

Front: chest smooth except for the last scattered strands of hair. Pectorals still thick with muscle but the skin over them   luminous. Soft. The expanded areolae dark against the lighter skin, the nipples protruding slightly from their centers. Below that, the stomach   still defined, the abdominal muscles visible, but a thin layer of new softness overlying them, the barest cushion that blurred the edges of the six-pack I'd never worked for and had always simply owned as a consequence of physical labor.

The waist. Visibly narrower when I stood straight and looked down at myself. The lines of my obliques pulling inward in a curve that continued into the hipbones   the bones that jutted more prominently now, wider, the architecture of my pelvis slowly expanding its wingspan. Below the hipbones, the crease of my groin   smoother, the coarse hair thinning, the skin there silk-textured and exquisitely sensitive.

My cock hung between my thighs. Unchanged. Present. I touched it   wrapped my hand around the shaft in the unthinking grip that every man performs a hundred times a day, adjusting, confirming, the casual maintenance of equipment you've owned since birth. The weight was the same. The girth. The length. The skin of it familiar against fingers that were, themselves, slightly different   smoother at the tips, the calluses thinning, the grip less rough.

I let it go. Turned sideways.

Profile: the narrowing was more dramatic from this angle. The taper from ribcage to waist to hip created a silhouette that was   I didn't want to say the word. But the word was there, hanging in the amber-dim air, refusing to be ignored. Curvier. The body in the mirror was curvier than my body had any right to be. The waist pulled in, the hips flared out, and the ass   I turned further, craning to see   was fuller. Rounder. The flat, muscular rear I'd sat on for twenty-six years was developing a new geometry, the gluteal muscles covered by that same thin layer of padding that was appearing everywhere, the overall effect a subtle but undeniable rounding that changed the line of my lower body from straight to curved.

I turned back to face the mirror. Looked at my face.

The jaw. Rounding at the corners. The stubble   ghosts. The lips   fuller, pinker, the Cupid's bow of the upper lip more defined than it had been this morning, as if someone were drawing it in with careful strokes. My cheekbones, always present but buried under the heavy masculine brow and jaw, were emerging   higher, more prominent, catching the thin light from the shutters.

I was still recognizable. Still me. Still the man I'd been   in the same way that a pencil sketch is recognizable as the person it depicts even as the artist erases lines and redraws them. The essential features were intact. The green eyes. The hooded lids. The crooked half-smile. The scarred eyebrow. All mine.

But the frame was changing. The container was being reshaped while the contents watched.

I touched my chest. Both hands, palms flat, the way I'd pressed them there this morning. The pectorals under my palms were   still muscle. Still thick. Still the product of a decade of sword work. But beneath the muscle, pressed against my ribcage, that new layer. Two millimeters, Nella had said. Two millimeters of tissue that was neither muscle nor fat but something specifically glandular, specifically purposeful, the cellular foundation of a change that would, if Nella's timeline was accurate, produce visible results within the week.

I pressed harder. Felt the give. The faintest sponginess beneath the firm muscle, the earliest intimation of softness where softness had no business being. The nipple under my right palm stiffened   the automatic, involuntary response to pressure   and the sensation lanced through me, a hot wire from nipple to navel, bright and sharp and unmistakable.

I let go. Stepped back from the mirror. Got dressed.

The tunic went on and the wool found the nipples and the sustained, low-level assault resumed   the fabric communicating with nerve endings that had been promoted overnight from background processors to front-line receptors. Every breath moved the tunic. Every movement of the tunic moved the nipples. Every movement of the nipples sent a whisper of sensation through my chest, and the whisper accumulated, minute by minute, into a persistent awareness that I was developing something behind those nipples that the tunic could feel even if the mirror couldn't see.

I sat by the window. Watched the market district. Waited for Mira. Tried to think about anything   anything   other than the fact that my body was building breasts.

[[Time passes|day_4_9]]

:: day_4_9

The room. The mirror. The late afternoon light   warmer, kinder, more forgiving than the gray dawn that had shown me the morning's inventory of changes. I stood in front of the polished tin again because I couldn't not. Because the body demanded to be witnessed, demanded that its changes be acknowledged, refused to be ignored.

/* <img src="img/scene/day_4/10.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" /> */
I pulled off the tunic and stood in the amber light and looked.

The afternoon told a different story than the dawn. In the warmer light, with the shadows softened, the changes that had seemed catastrophic at five in the morning looked   not less real. But less alien. The smoother skin caught the golden light and held it, glowing faintly, the warm tone of it more evident in this softer illumination. The sparse remnants of chest hair were barely visible against skin that looked   healthy. Clean. Young in a way I hadn't been since before I picked up a sword.

My face. I leaned close. The jawline was   softer. I could see it now, in the more flattering light, the comparison clearer because I'd been studying my face for three days straight. The square corners of my jaw were rounding. Not dramatically   not feminine yet, not curved   but the hard right angles that had defined my lower face were easing, the bone beneath the skin filing itself down into something less angular. Less blunt.

My lips were fuller. Both of them, but especially the lower one   always my thicker lip, the one that gave my resting expression its slightly petulant quality. It was plumper now, pinker, the skin of it softer, the border between lip and skin more defined. When I pressed my lips together, they sat differently against each other. More cushion. More give.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
This is happening.
<</dialog>>

I said it without emotion. A statement of fact. The sky is blue. Water is wet. My lips are fuller than yesterday. Baseline reality had expanded to include things it had never previously contained, and the only way to process the expansion was to narrate it, to speak the changes into existence because silence gave them too much power.

The scar through my left eyebrow. Still there. Thin, pale, raised. The same scar. The same tiny notch of missing hair in the dark brow above my left eye.

I pressed my thumb against it. Felt the familiar ridge of healed tissue, the slight catch where the knife had parted the skin at twenty-one. A bar fight. A scar without a good story. The most ordinary mark on an increasingly extraordinary body.

It remained. Whatever the compound was changing, whatever systems it was rewriting, the scar stayed. Small and stubborn and mine.

Mira came in from the hallway. She'd been downstairs, settling our tab in advance   paying for three days of room whether we used them or not, a deposit against our return from the road. She stopped in the doorway and looked at me. Standing in the late light. Shirtless. The changed chest, the thinning hair, the smoothing skin catching gold.

She said nothing for a long moment. Her amber eyes moved over me with the slow, thorough attention she usually reserved for new terrain   mapping, cataloguing, filing the data for future reference.

<<dialog "Mira">>
You're losing mass in the upper body. The shoulders are slightly narrower than this morning.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You can see that?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I know your shoulders the way I know my bow. I know their width. I know their line. They're narrower. By a fraction   maybe a quarter inch. But I can see it.
<</dialog>>

She crossed the room. Stood behind me. Put her hands on my shoulders   one on each, her palms resting against the deltoid, her fingers curling over the top. The weight of her hands was familiar. The landscape they rested on was not.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Still solid. Still you. Just   a slightly smaller version of you.
<</dialog>>

Her hands slid down. Over my shoulder blades. Along the line of my ribcage, which she touched with the gentle, exploratory attention of someone reading terrain with their fingertips. Down to my waist. She spanned it with her hands   thumbs nearly meeting at my spine, fingers reaching toward the midline of my belly. The gesture was intimate and diagnostic simultaneously. She was measuring me. Holding the dimensions of my changing body in her hands like a potter assessing clay.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Narrower. Definitely narrower. By at least an inch from Day 0 and the skin here  
<</dialog>>

Her thumbs traced circles on my lower back. The sensation was disproportionate   her calloused thumbpads against skin that received the touch like a shout, every ridge of her fingerprints registering with vivid, specific clarity.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Everything feels   louder. Every touch is more. I can feel the grain of the wood through my boots. I can feel individual threads in the blanket and when you touch me  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Like this?
<</dialog>>

Her hands moved to my hips. Rested on the bones   the bones that were widening, migrating outward in their slow skeletal drift. Her fingers found the new angle and she pressed, gently, testing the architecture.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Wider. I can feel it. The bone is   it's moving, isn't it? Actually physically widening.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That's what Nella said. Skeletal restructuring. One of the thirty-day milestones.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Does it hurt?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Not pain. An ache. Deep. Like growing pains. Remember growing pains? When you were a kid and your legs hurt at night because the bones were getting longer?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Half-elf. We don't get growing pains. We just grow. Efficiently. Without the drama.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Of course you don't.
<</dialog>>

She rested her chin on my shoulder. We stood together in front of the mirror   me shirtless, changed, the amber light painting my new skin in gold, and Mira behind me, her face beside mine, her eyes meeting my eyes in the warped reflection.

<<dialog "Mira">>
We're going to fix this.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I know.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We're going to find Ysolde and we're going to get the anti-compound and we're going to put you back. I promise you.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You never make promises.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm making one now. Take it before I change my mind.
<</dialog>>

I took it. Held it the way you hold a coin in a storm   tight, close, aware that the wind could steal it at any moment.

<<addobjective "day_4" "Go downstairs for dinner and drinks">>
<<set $day_4_10 = false>>
[[Go downstairs|day_4_10]]

:: day_4_10
<<set $day_4_10 = true>>

Evening at the Broken Antler. Our last night in Valdmere before the road. The common room was busy   rest-day crowd, the same loud, ale-soaked normalcy that had been the backdrop of our entire stay in this city. The bard was back, assaulting a different ballad with the same incompetence. The card players were losing different amounts of money. The orange cat was asleep in its favorite spot on the bar, one paw draped over the edge, as indifferent to human suffering as every cat that had ever lived.

We took our booth. Backs to the wall. Eyes on the door.

/* <img src="img/scene/day_4/11.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" /> */
The barmaid came over. Not Lenna   a different girl. Tess. Short, stocky, with a broad face and hands that could carry four tankards without spilling a drop. She'd been serving us all week and had settled into the comfortable familiarity of a woman who knew her regulars and their orders and didn't need conversation to maintain the transaction.

<<dialog "Tess">>
Evening. Two ales? Or the full pitcher tonight?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Pitcher and two bowls of whatever's in the pot.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Tess">>
Stew again. I think it's the same stew, honestly. Just added water and a carrot. The carrot is new.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The carrot will be the highlight of the evening.
<</dialog>>

Tess turned to go, then paused. Looked at me   a double-take, the kind of reflexive second look that people give when something in their peripheral vision doesn't match their expectations. Her eyes moved over my face with the quick assessment of a woman who'd been serving drinks to this particular face for a week and was recalibrating her mental image.

<<dialog "Tess">>
You look different.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Different how?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Tess">>
I don't know. Just   different. Rested, maybe? Your skin looks clearer and your jaw   have you been shaving? You usually have the scruff going on.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
New soap. From the market. The fancy stuff.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Tess">>
Looks good on you. Whatever it is.
<</dialog>>

She left. I stared at the table.

<<dialog "Mira">>
That was smooth.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That was terrifying.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
She noticed the stubble and the skin. Nothing else. The rest is hidden under the tunic. We're fine.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
For now. What about Day 5? Day 7? Day 10, when the   when things become visible through clothing?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
By Day 5, we'll be on the road. No barmaids. No mirrors. Just the forest and the surveyor and whatever goblins haven't learned their lesson from last time.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Marsh? The surveyor? What happens when he notices?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Marsh is a Consortium assessor who catalogs ruins. He notices stonework and load-bearing walls. He's not going to be paying attention to your jawline.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You don't know that.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I know that men who spend their lives studying architectural collapse are generally not in the habit of studying other men's facial bone structure. Eat your stew. The new carrot isn't going to eat itself.
<</dialog>>

Tess returned with the pitcher and bowls. She set them down with the practiced economy of motion that good barmaids develop   everything placed, nothing spilled, the transaction complete in under three seconds. But her hand brushed mine as she set down the bowl, an accidental contact, and I saw her reaction   a tiny flinch, a micro-expression of surprise that crossed her face and vanished.

My hand. She'd felt the skin of my hand   the back of it, where the hair was nearly gone, where the rough weathered texture had been replaced by something smoother and softer. A small thing. A second of contact. But she'd felt it, and whatever her fingertips told her brain didn't match the hand of the man she'd been serving ale to all week.

She said nothing. Went back to the bar. Didn't look back.

<<dialog "Mira">>
She felt your hand.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I know.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Start wearing gloves. On the road, it won't look strange. Sellswords wear riding gloves.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I don't own riding gloves.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Then we buy some in the morning. Before we leave. Add it to the list.
<</dialog>>

She poured the ale. The pitcher was generous. The ale was terrible. The stew was   it had a carrot. Tess wasn't lying about that. The carrot sat in the gray broth like a beacon of nutritional hope, the single ingredient in the bowl that had once been a living plant and retained some dim memory of the experience.

I ate. The flavors hit with the amplified clarity I was getting used to   each component of the stew rendered in high definition by taste buds that had been recalibrated overnight. The salt was sharper. The meat was gamier. The carrot was, genuinely, the best thing in the bowl   sweet and earthy and real in a way the rest of the meal could only aspire to.

Three tankards in, something loosened. Not the anxiety   that lived deeper, in a place that ale couldn't reach. But the surface tension. The clenched-jaw, hunched-shoulder rigidity that I'd been carrying since dawn, the physical manifestation of a man trying to hold his body together through sheer will. The ale softened it. Rounded the edges. Let my shoulders drop and my spine settle and my breathing deepen into something that almost resembled calm.

Mira's knee pressed against mine under the table. Not accidentally   deliberately. The warm pressure of her leg against my leg, the solid fact of her beside me. She left it there.

<<dialog "Mira">>
To the road.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
To the road.
<</dialog>>

We drank. The ale was terrible. Reliably, consistently, magnificently terrible. I tasted it more vividly than I'd ever tasted it before   the specific chemistry of bad hops, worse water, and whatever unnamed ingredient gave it its characteristic flavor of institutional disappointment. My enhanced senses were, in this particular application, a curse.

Three tankards in, the sharp edges began to file down. The anxiety that had been sitting in my chest since dawn   a cold, clenched fist of worry   loosened its grip by fractions. The common room's noise became less assault and more ambiance. The fire in the hearth threw warm shadows across the low ceiling, and the particular comfort of a bad tavern on a cold night wrapped around me like a blanket that smelled of smoke and poor decisions.

Mira's knee pressed against mine under the table. Not accidentally   deliberately. The warm pressure of her leg against my leg, the solid fact of her beside me. She left it there.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Last night in a bed for a while. The road doesn't have mattresses.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The road doesn't have stew this bad, either. Trade-offs.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The road has goblins.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Trade-offs.
<</dialog>>

She refilled our tankards. The pitcher was half-empty. The evening was deepening, the light from the windows fading to blue-gray as the sun dropped below the city's roofline. The candles and hearth fire took over, painting everything in warm amber.

Mira's hand found my thigh under the table. Rested there. Her thumb traced a slow circle on the inside of my leg, just above the knee, and the sensation   the amplified, unbearable sensitivity   shot upward through my thigh and into my groin with a directness that made me shift in my seat.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Let's go upstairs.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Now?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Now. I want to feel you the way you are now.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The way I am now is in the middle of turning into  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The way you are now is exactly what I said. You. Now. Tonight. Before the road changes things. Before tomorrow adds whatever tomorrow adds. I want tonight's version of you. While I can still have him.
<</dialog>>

Her thumb pressed harder against my inner thigh. The circle widened. The heat of her hand through the fabric was a pulse that my newly sensitive skin received like a signal, amplified and broadcast through nerve pathways that were wider, more responsive, more alive than they'd been a week ago.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Upstairs.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Upstairs.
<</dialog>>

[[Go upstairs|day_4_11]]

:: day_4_11

She closed the door. Threw the bolt. Turned to face me in the dim room   one candle lit, the flame small and warm, the light barely enough to see by. Enough to see shapes. Outlines. The suggestion of a body in the dark.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Take the tunic off. I want to see.
<</dialog>>

